## Proof (Post–Widder inversion via the Gamma distribution)

**Setup.** Let $h$ be bounded and continuous on $[0,\infty)$, with Laplace transform
$$L_h(\lambda)=\int_0^\infty h(x)\,e^{-\lambda x}\,dx,\qquad \lambda>0.$$
Let $Y_1,Y_2,\dots$ be i.i.d. exponential random variables of rate $1/z$ (so $Y_i\sim\mathrm{Exp}(1/z)$, density $\frac{1}{z}e^{-x/z}$, mean $\mathbb{E}Y_i=z$). Set
$$S_n:=Y_1+\cdots+Y_n.$$

---

### Step 1. Distribution of $S_n$

The sum of $n$ i.i.d. $\mathrm{Exp}(1/z)$ variables is $\mathrm{Gamma}(n,\,1/z)$ (shape $n$, rate $1/z$). Since $\Gamma(n)=(n-1)!$, its density is
$$f_{S_n}(x)=\frac{(1/z)^n}{\Gamma(n)}\,x^{n-1}e^{-x/z}=\frac{x^{n-1}e^{-x/z}}{z^n\,(n-1)!},\qquad x>0.$$

### Step 2. Law of Large Numbers gives the limit

Since the $Y_i$ are i.i.d. with $\mathbb{E}Y_1=z$, the strong law yields
$$\frac{S_n}{n}\xrightarrow{\text{a.s.}}z\qquad(n\to\infty).$$
Because $h$ is continuous, $h(S_n/n)\xrightarrow{\text{a.s.}}h(z)$. Because $h$ is **bounded**, $|h(S_n/n)|\le\|h\|_\infty$, so the dominated (bounded) convergence theorem gives
$$\mathbb{E}\!\left[h\!\left(\frac{S_n}{n}\right)\right]\xrightarrow{n\to\infty}h(z).\tag{$\star$}$$

### Step 3. Express the expectation as an integral

$$\mathbb{E}\!\left[h\!\left(\frac{S_n}{n}\right)\right]=\int_0^\infty h\!\left(\frac{x}{n}\right)\frac{x^{n-1}e^{-x/z}}{z^n\,(n-1)!}\,dx.$$

Substitute $u=x/n$ (so $x=nu,\;dx=n\,du$):
$$=\int_0^\infty h(u)\,\frac{(nu)^{n-1}e^{-nu/z}}{z^n\,(n-1)!}\,n\,du
=\frac{n^n}{z^n\,(n-1)!}\int_0^\infty h(u)\,u^{n-1}e^{-(n/z)u}\,du.\tag{1}$$

### Step 4. Relate the integral to $L_h^{(n-1)}$

Because $h$ is bounded, we may differentiate under the integral sign any number of times:
$$L_h^{(k)}(\lambda)=\frac{d^k}{d\lambda^k}\int_0^\infty h(u)e^{-\lambda u}\,du=\int_0^\infty h(u)\,(-u)^k e^{-\lambda u}\,du=(-1)^k\int_0^\infty h(u)\,u^k e^{-\lambda u}\,du.$$

Taking $k=n-1$ and $\lambda=n/z$:
$$L_h^{(n-1)}\!\left(\frac{n}{z}\right)=(-1)^{n-1}\int_0^\infty h(u)\,u^{n-1}e^{-(n/z)u}\,du,$$
so that
$$\int_0^\infty h(u)\,u^{n-1}e^{-(n/z)u}\,du=(-1)^{n-1}\,L_h^{(n-1)}\!\left(\frac{n}{z}\right).\tag{2}$$

### Step 5. Combine

Substituting (2) into (1):
$$\mathbb{E}\!\left[h\!\left(\frac{S_n}{n}\right)\right]=\frac{n^n}{z^n\,(n-1)!}\,(-1)^{n-1}\,L_h^{(n-1)}\!\left(\frac{n}{z}\right)=(-1)^{n-1}\frac{\left(\frac{n}{z}\right)^n L_h^{(n-1)}\!\left(\frac{n}{z}\right)}{(n-1)!}.$$

Taking $n\to\infty$ and using $(\star)$:

$$\boxed{\,h(z)=\lim_{n\to\infty}(-1)^{n-1}\frac{\left(\frac{n}{z}\right)^n L_h^{(n-1)}\!\left(\frac{n}{z}\right)}{(n-1)!}\,}.$$

---

**Summary of the argument.** The sum $S_n$ of $n$ i.i.d. $\mathrm{Exp}(1/z)$ variables is $\mathrm{Gamma}(n,1/z)$. By the strong law of large numbers $S_n/n\to z$ a.s., and boundedness of $h$ upgrades this to $\mathbb{E}[h(S_n/n)]\to h(z)$. Writing out this expectation with the Gamma density and changing variables $u=x/n$ produces an integral of $h(u)\,u^{n-1}e^{-(n/z)u}$, which is exactly $(-1)^{n-1}L_h^{(n-1)}(n/z)$ (differentiation under the integral, justified by boundedness of $h$). Equating the two expressions for $\mathbb{E}[h(S_n/n)]$ and passing to the limit yields the Post–Widder inversion formula. $\blacksquare$

